ef 17592186045417] [17592186557138 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557138 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557138 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557138 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557138 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186556617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557138 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7d11b4fd-cfd0-5513-851a-592175c62665" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557153 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557153 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557153 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557153 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186557014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557153 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186557045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557153 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557153 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557153 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186557014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557153 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "977b55e3-a317-5bd9-b4ac-64fc7f8737d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557174 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557174 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557174 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557174 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186557023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557174 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186557032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557174 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557174 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557174 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186557023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557174 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c0e2b3fe-1fd8-5dda-acb7-cbb560deb0ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557198 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557198 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557198 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557198 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557198 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557198 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557198 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557198 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186556504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557198 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "eaca67a9-c11b-5ef7-9bd5-650d3e06d0b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557222 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557222 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95396" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557222 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557222 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}-\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{16}.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)=24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $25{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{2}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557222 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "27762923-ddbc-55ca-b261-ca5c61feb064" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557222 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}-\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{16}\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}-\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2} & =-\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ 25{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{2}^{2}-\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2})+2\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2}) & =-\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ 25{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{2}^{2}+5{y}_{2} & =-\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{2} & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{2} & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{2}) & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{64}-\\frac{1}{64}) & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{64})-\\frac{4}{64} & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{8})^{2}-\\frac{1}{16} & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{8})^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{8},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{z}_{1}^{2}+4{z}_{2}^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{\\frac{1}{5}}+\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{\\frac{1}{4}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}})^{2}}+\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{(\\frac{1}{2})^{2}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nA sum of squares is equal to zero if and only if each summand is zero, so the given quadratic equation represents a single point." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557250 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557250 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95870" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557250 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557250 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following equations defines a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive ${x}_{1}$-axis,  in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557250 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4e624380-04d1-5614-ba5a-b0ac9bddf8d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557250 #edb/ref 1074 "The correct answer is \n\n$$\n{C}_{3}\\,:\\,3{x}_{2}^{2}={x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis equation defines a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive ${x}_{1}$-axis.\n\nNotice that the other equations do not define a parabola:\n\n- ${C}_{1}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=0$ defines a single point $(0,0)$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\n- ${C}_{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{9}=1$ is the equation of a hyperbola with semiaxes $2$ and $3.$\n\n- ${C}_{4}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}=4$ represents two parallel lines in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane: ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{1}=-2.$\n\n- ${C}_{5}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{4}=-1$ has no points in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane that satisfy the equation (imaginary ellipse)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557253 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557253 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95392" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557253 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557253 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=\\frac{119}{8}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557253 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "584e6ba7-6a66-5994-b1b0-75bfa6a71313" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557253 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=\\frac{119}{8}\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2})+\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2}) & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2}+5{y}_{1} & =\\frac{119}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2}+5{y}_{1} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2({y}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{1})-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2({y}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{1}+\\frac{25}{16}-\\frac{25}{16})-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2{({y}_{1}+\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{8}-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2{({y}_{1}+\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}+\\frac{5}{4}, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{z}_{1}^{2}-3{z}_{2}^{2} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents a hyperbola with semiaxes $3$ and $\\sqrt{6}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557300 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557300 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140553" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557300 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557300 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following equations defines two intersecting lines in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557300 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c0f59d2f-ea36-559d-85fe-96af7c52c659" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557300 #edb/ref 1074 "The correct answer is \n\n$$\n{C}_{3}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{3}-{x}_{2}^{2}=0.\n$$\n\nThis equation defines two intersecting lines, ${x}_{1}=\\sqrt{3}{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{1}=-\\sqrt{3}{x}_{2},$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\nNotice that the other equations do not define two parallel lines:\n\n- ${C}_{1}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{3}-{x}_{2}^{2}=1$ is the equation of a hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $1.$\n\n- ${C}_{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{3}+{x}_{2}^{2}=0$ defines a single point $(0,0)$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\n- ${C}_{4}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}=3$ defines two parallel lines, ${x}_{1}=\\sqrt{3}$ and ${x}_{1}=-\\sqrt{3},$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\n- ${C}_{5}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{3}+{x}_{2}^{2}=1$ is the equation of an ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557324 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557324 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96069" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557324 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557324 #edb/ref 1071 "What type of quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-4{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=13\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557324 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f46d525e-1765-5c37-bb92-276f989431a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557324 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we complete the squares for the variables ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-4{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2} & =13 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1})-3({x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}) & =13 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}+1-1)-3({x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}+1-1) & =13 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}-1)^{2}-2-3({x}_{2}-1)^{2}+3 & =13 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}-1)^{2}-3({x}_{2}-1)^{2} & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}-1 \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{6}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{6}$ and $2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557330 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557330 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96091" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557330 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557330 #edb/ref 1071 "What type of quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557330 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f72287c6-b1eb-590a-839e-db294cf79f90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557330 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we complete the squares for the variable ${x}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{1} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2({x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{1})+2{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2({x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{1}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})+2{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2{({x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-2{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2{({x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2{({x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {({x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a circle of radius $1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
